Mechanical & Structural Neuropathic Syndromes (Herniated Disc/Spinal Stenosis Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Surgical Neuroma Mortons neuroma)
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Anatomical radiating nerve pain, Dermatomal sensory/motor deficit, Positive mechanical provocation signs

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-023
Reason for Visit: Excruciating, electric-shock-like pain shooting down the right leg, rendering the patient completely immobile.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented severe, unremitting radicular pain associated with a new inability to weight-bear and subjective numbness. Urgent in-person assessment is required to definitively exclude Cauda Equina Syndrome (a neurosurgical emergency) and to provide potent, immediate analgesia for an acute mechanical nerve root compression before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "A lightning bolt of pain shoots from my right buttock all the way down to my big toe if I move even an inch. I can't walk to the bathroom."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an acute onset of sharp, shooting, intense electric-shock-like pain directly radiating along the precise anatomical course of the right sciatic nerve (L5/S1 territory). The pain began suddenly after lifting a heavy box yesterday. There is severe paresthesia and a distinct burning sensation strictly localized within the L5 dermatome (lateral calf, dorsum of foot). There is a marked exacerbation of the severe neuropathic pain strictly triggered by targeted mechanical compression, specifically coughing, sneezing, or attempting to bend forward. The patient complains of subjective weakness in the right foot and objective sensory loss mapped to the L5 distribution. There is a distinct relief of painful symptoms achieved only by actively resting completely flat on the floor with knees bent.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound on the floor; completely unable to weight-bear or sit upright.
Social/Safety Concerns: Inability to access toilet facilities; severe pain causing extreme distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/95 mmHg (pain-driven) | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 92 Kg
General: Lying supine on the living room floor, intensely guarding the right leg, grimacing in pain.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals anatomical radiating nerve pain. There is a strongly positive straight leg raise test (Lasegue's sign) on the right at 30 degrees, reproducing severe electric pain down to the foot (positive mechanical provocation sign). Crossed straight leg raise is negative. Dermatomal sensory deficit to light touch is present over the right lateral calf and dorsum of the foot (L5). Motor examination reveals mild weakness in right extensor hallucis longus (power 4/5). Diminished right medial hamstring reflex. Crucially, perineal/saddle sensation is completely intact, and rectal sphincter tone is strictly normal. No urinary retention or incontinence.
Environmental Observations: Patient resting on a firm yoga mat; heavy lifting boxes remain in the hallway.
Medication Reconciliation: Ibuprofen 400mg and Paracetamol 1000mg taken with zero effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Lumbar Radiculopathy (Right L5, suspected acute posterolateral disc herniation) - Status: Acute Severe Pain Crisis
Differentials:

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact saddle sensation, normal anal sphincter tone, and absence of bowel/bladder dysfunction or bilateral severe leg weakness.

Piriformis Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain explicitly worsened by Valsalva maneuvers (coughing/sneezing) and the presence of objective L5 motor weakness (extensor hallucis longus), pointing to a spinal root rather than peripheral entrapment.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is shooting/electrical rather than a constant deep ache, negative Homan's sign, normal calf circumference, and positive neural tension signs.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is immobilized by an acute, severe radicular pain crisis secondary to a suspected acute lumbar disc herniation. The severity of the pain and the new subjective weakness triggered immediate concern for a structural neurological emergency. Deferring assessment would result in agonizing, unmanaged pain and leave the critical question of Cauda Equina Syndrome unanswered, posing a massive medicolegal and clinical risk. Urgent after-hours physical examination definitively ruled out central cord/cauda equina compression, allowing for safe, aggressive outpatient pharmacological management (opioids/neuropathic agents) and immediate biomechanical offloading strategies to restore basic function.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Tapentadol 50mg orally STAT. Prescribed a short course of Tapentadol 50mg PRN for severe breakthrough pain and Pregabalin 75mg at night for radicular nerve pain. Advised strict, short-term relative rest.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Step 1: By lying flat on the floor with a thick pillow under the knees (the psoas position), the patient mechanically flattens the lumbar spine, immediately opening the nerve foramina and relieving the acute root compression.

Step 2: By avoiding all forward bending, twisting, or lifting, the patient prevents further extrusion of the disc material, stopping the continuous mechanical trauma to the inflamed nerve.

Step 3: By taking the neuropathic medication consistently at night, the patient artificially calms the highly sensitized nerve root, guaranteeing enough sleep to tolerate the healing process.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to musculoskeletal physiotherapy once acute pain subsides (approx. 3-5 days).
DME/Safety (Optional): Urinal bottle provided for immediate night use to minimize painful mobilization.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 3 days to arrange a targeted lumbar MRI if motor weakness progresses.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed immediately to the Emergency Department if you develop numbness between your legs (saddle area), lose control of your bowel or bladder, or completely lose the ability to lift your foot (foot drop).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; an MRI is only required if surgery is contemplated or "red flag" symptoms develop.
Clinical Course: The acute, agonizing pain will begin to dull within a few days with the strong medications, but the nerve irritation will likely take 4-8 weeks to fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for urgent review if the pain completely stops responding to the new medications or if the numbness spreads up the other leg.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A spinal disc has bulged and is directly crushing the main nerve running down your leg. The severe pain is the nerve screaming. The new medications include a strong painkiller and a specific drug to numb the angry nerve. You must keep the spine perfectly neutral to let the inflammation shrink.
Actionable Steps: Rest in the knees-bent position. Do not sit in soft couches. Take the medications exactly as prescribed.

